If a rod of length <span class="math-tex">\(3\)</span> m with its mass acting per unit length, is

directly proportional to distance <span class="math-tex">\(x\)</span> from one of its ends, then its centre of gravity from that end will be at:&nbsp;
<table border="1" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="1" style="min-width: 300px;"><tbody><tr><td>1.</td><td><span class="math-tex">\(1.5\)</span> m</td><td>2.</td><td><span class="math-tex">\(2\)</span> m</td></tr><tr><td>3.</td><td><span class="math-tex">\(2.5\)</span> m</td><td>4.</td><td><span class="math-tex">\(3.0\)</span> m</td></tr></tbody></table>

Given below are two statements:&nbsp;
<table border="1" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="1"><tbody><tr><td><strong>Assertion&nbsp;(A):</strong></td><td>If there is no external torque on a body about its centre of mass, then the velocity of the centre of mass remains constant.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reason&nbsp;(R):</strong></td><td>The linear momentum of an isolated system remains constant.</td></tr></tbody></table>
&nbsp;

<table border="1" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="1"><tbody><tr><td>1.</td><td>Both&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;are True and&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;is the correct explanation of&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>2.</td><td>Both&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;are True but&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;is not the correct explanation of&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>3.</td><td><strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;is True but&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;is False.</td></tr><tr><td>4.</td><td><strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;is False but&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;is True.</td></tr></tbody></table>

Given below are two statements:&nbsp;
<table border="1" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="1"><tbody><tr><td><strong>Assertion&nbsp;(A):</strong></td><td>If the ice on the polar caps of the earth melts, then the length of the day will increase.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reason&nbsp;(R):</strong></td><td>Moment of inertia of the earth increases, as ice on polar caps melts.</td></tr></tbody></table>
&nbsp;

<table border="1" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="1"><tbody><tr><td>1.</td><td>Both&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;are True and&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;is the correct explanation of&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>2.</td><td>Both&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;are True but&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;is not the correct explanation of&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>3.</td><td><strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;is True but&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;is False.</td></tr><tr><td>4.</td><td><strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;is False but&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;is True.</td></tr></tbody></table>

